    {
      "title": "Module 1 \u00b7 Rules of Surds",
      "html": "<p>A <b>surd</b> is an irrational root such as $\\sqrt 2$, $\\sqrt 3$, $\\sqrt[3]{5}$.</p>$$\\sqrt a \\cdot \\sqrt b = \\sqrt{ab},\\qquad \\sqrt{\\dfrac{a}{b}} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt a}{\\sqrt b},\\qquad k\\sqrt a + m\\sqrt a = (k+m)\\sqrt a$$<p>Simplify by extracting perfect squares: $\\sqrt{50} = \\sqrt{25\\cdot 2} = 5\\sqrt 2$.</p><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Like surds: coefficients add, so result is $13\\sqrt{3}$.</li><li>Like surds: coefficients add, so result is $13\\sqrt{3}$.</li></ul><h4>Worked example</h4><p><b>Q:</b> Simplify $5\\sqrt{3} + 8\\sqrt{3}$.<br/><b>A:</b> 13\\sqrt{3}</p><p><b>Q:</b> Simplify $4\\sqrt{3} + 9\\sqrt{3}$.<br/><b>A:</b> 13\\sqrt{3}</p>",
      "examples": [
        {
          "q": "Simplify $\\sqrt{72}$.",
          "a": "$\\sqrt{72} = \\sqrt{36\\cdot 2} = 6\\sqrt 2$."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Module 2 \u00b7 Rationalizing Denominators",
      "html": "<p>Multiply numerator and denominator by the surd (or its conjugate) to remove the surd from the denominator.</p>$$\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt a} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\sqrt a}{\\sqrt a} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt a}{a},\\qquad \\dfrac{1}{a+\\sqrt b}\\cdot \\dfrac{a-\\sqrt b}{a-\\sqrt b} = \\dfrac{a-\\sqrt b}{a^2-b}$$<h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>$\\sqrt{216} = \\sqrt{36\\cdot 6} = 6\\sqrt{6}$.</li><li>$\\sqrt{275} = \\sqrt{25\\cdot 11} = 5\\sqrt{11}$.</li></ul><h4>Worked example</h4><p><b>Q:</b> Simplify $\\sqrt{216}$.<br/><b>A:</b> 6\\sqrt{6}</p><p><b>Q:</b> Simplify $\\sqrt{275}$.<br/><b>A:</b> 5\\sqrt{11}</p>",
      "examples": [
        {
          "q": "Rationalize $\\dfrac{3}{\\sqrt 2}$.",
          "a": "$\\dfrac{3}{\\sqrt 2}\\cdot\\dfrac{\\sqrt 2}{\\sqrt 2} = \\dfrac{3\\sqrt 2}{2}$."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Module 3 \u00b7 Conjugate Surds",
      "html": "<p>The <b>conjugate</b> of $a+\\sqrt b$ is $a-\\sqrt b$. Their product $(a+\\sqrt b)(a-\\sqrt b) = a^2 - b$ is rational.</p><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Multiply top and bottom by conjugate $(1-\\sqrt{2})$; denominator becomes $1^2-2=-1$.</li><li>Multiply top and bottom by conjugate $(4-\\sqrt{5})$; denominator becomes $4^2-5=11$.</li></ul><h4>Worked example</h4><p><b>Q:</b> Rationalize $\\dfrac{1}{1+\\sqrt{2}}$.<br/><b>A:</b> \\dfrac{1-\\sqrt{2}}{-1}</p><p><b>Q:</b> Rationalize $\\dfrac{1}{4+\\sqrt{5}}$.<br/><b>A:</b> \\dfrac{4-\\sqrt{5}}{11}</p>",
      "examples": [
        {
          "q": "Rationalize $\\dfrac{1}{2+\\sqrt 3}$.",
          "a": "Multiply by $\\tfrac{2-\\sqrt 3}{2-\\sqrt 3}$: $\\dfrac{2-\\sqrt 3}{4-3} = 2-\\sqrt 3$."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Revision and exam focus",
      "html": "<p>Revise the whole topic by working through its key points in order, then test yourself with the 50-question quiz for this topic. Pay attention to definitions, differences between similar terms, and questions that ask you to apply the topic rather than only recall it.</p><h4>Worked examples</h4><p><b>Q:</b> Simplify $\\sqrt{216}$.<br/><b>A:</b> 6\\sqrt{6} \u2014 $\\sqrt{216} = \\sqrt{36\\cdot 6} = 6\\sqrt{6}$.</p><p><b>Q:</b> Simplify $\\sqrt{275}$.<br/><b>A:</b> 5\\sqrt{11} \u2014 $\\sqrt{275} = \\sqrt{25\\cdot 11} = 5\\sqrt{11}$.</p><p><b>Q:</b> Simplify $\\sqrt{343}$.<br/><b>A:</b> 7\\sqrt{7} \u2014 $\\sqrt{343} = \\sqrt{49\\cdot 7} = 7\\sqrt{7}$.</p>",
